      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "In order to calculate the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we can use the multiplication rule for independent events.\n\nEach day has a probability $P(S)$ of rain, and the probability of having no rain on Saturday and Sunday is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{No rain on Saturday}) = 1 - P(S) = 1 - \\frac{60}{100} = \\frac{40}{100}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for Sunday:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{No rain on Sunday}) = 1 - P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 1 - \\frac{25}{100} = \\frac{75}{100}\n\\]\n\nThe probability that it will rain on both days is the product of the probabilities of rain and no rain for each day:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = P(S) \\times P(\\text{No rain on Sunday})\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = \\frac{60}{100} \\times \\frac{75}{100} = \\frac{60 \\times 75}{100 \\times 100} = \\frac{4500}{10000}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability it will rain on both days, expressed as a percent, is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{45}\n\\]",
