      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the fact that the probability of two independent events occurring together is the product of their individual probabilities. \n\nGiven:\n- The probability it will rain on Saturday ($P(\\text{Rain on Saturday})$) is 60%, which can be written as 0.60.\n- The probability it will rain on Sunday ($P(\\text{Rain on Sunday})$) is 25%, which can be written as 0.25.\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is:\n\\[ P(\\text{Rain on both Saturday and Sunday}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15. \\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\\[ 0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%. \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
