      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, given that the probability of rain on Saturday is 60% and the probability of rain on Sunday is 25%. Since the probability of rain on each day is independent, we can find the combined probability of rain on both days by multiplying the individual probabilities.\n\n1. Identify the probability of rain on Saturday.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) = 60\\% = 0.60\n   \\]\n\n2. Identify the probability of rain on Sunday.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 25\\% = 0.25\n   \\]\n\n3. Since the events are independent, the probability of rain on both days is the product of the individual probabilities.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Rain on both Saturday and Sunday}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.60 \\times 0.25\n   \\]\n\n4. Calculate the product.\n   \\[\n   0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n   \\]\n\n5. Convert the probability to a percentage.\n   \\[\n   0.15 = 15\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
